---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="About" description="Doug Rosenberg — engineer with 3+ years building enterprise systems (ERPs, Fortune 10 platforms), writing at Haxbyte about applying that same discipline to smaller, faster-moving projects.">
	<div class="about-container">
		<h1>About Haxbyte</h1>

		<section class="about-section">
			<h2>Who I Am</h2>
			<p>
				I'm Doug Rosenberg. I spent 3+ years building enterprise systems—ERPs for global nonprofits,
				platforms for Fortune 10 companies, complex full-stack applications for manufacturers. I led
				architecture sessions, designed scalable databases, and shipped systems that handled thousands
				of concurrent users.
			</p>
			<p>
				Then I built two Squarespace sites for small businesses, on the side. It was the same
				discipline at a different scale, and it got me thinking about the gap: what enterprise-grade
				practices actually look like once you strip away the org chart and the ceremony.
			</p>
			<p>
				Haxbyte is where I write about that gap — clean code, fast performance, architecture that
				doesn't turn into technical debt, regardless of the project's size.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="about-section">
			<h2>What I Write About</h2>
			<p>
				Build logs and technical notes, held to the same standard I'd apply to a system running
				production traffic:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Build-in-public posts</strong> — real projects, real decisions, including the ones that didn't work</li>
				<li><strong>Architecture & performance</strong> — chasing sub-2-second loads and structure that scales</li>
				<li><strong>Accessibility</strong> — WCAG 2.1 AA as a baseline, not an afterthought</li>
				<li><strong>Tools & process</strong> — what's actually worth adopting versus what's just noise</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="about-section">
			<h2>The Haxbyte Approach</h2>
			<p>
				Enterprise discipline, applied to smaller, faster-moving projects.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Clean, readable code</strong> — Doesn't become technical debt. The next person touching it (often future me) can understand and maintain it.</li>
				<li><strong>Mobile-first design</strong> — Built for how people actually use the web, not retrofitted after the fact.</li>
				<li><strong>Performance obsession</strong> — Sub-2-second loads. No bloat. No unnecessary frameworks.</li>
				<li><strong>Accessibility built in</strong> — WCAG 2.1 AA baseline, on every device.</li>
				<li><strong>Architecture that scales</strong> — Grows with the project instead of forcing a rewrite.</li>
				<li><strong>Security from the ground up</strong> — HTTPS, secure by default, no shortcuts or workarounds.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				No templates. No AI-generated design shortcuts. No vendor lock-in. Just intentional work built to last.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="about-section cta-section">
			<h2>Let's Talk</h2>
			<p>
				Want to talk shop, compare notes on a build, or just say hi? I'm easy to find.
			</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="cta-button">Get in touch</a>
		</section>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.about-container {
		max-width: var(--max-width-prose);
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: var(--space-3xl) var(--space-md);
	}

	.about-container h1 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.about-section {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-3xl);
		padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
	}

	.about-section:last-child {
		border-bottom: none;
	}

	.about-section h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.about-section ul {
		list-style: none;
		padding-left: 0;
	}

	.about-section li {
		position: relative;
		padding-left: var(--space-lg);
		margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
	}

	.about-section li::before {
		content: '→';
		position: absolute;
		left: 0;
		color: var(--spark);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.cta-section {
		text-align: center;
	}

	.cta-button {
		display: inline-block;
		background-color: var(--link-orange);
		color: var(--white);
		padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
		border-radius: var(--radius-md);
		text-decoration: none;
		font-weight: 600;
		transition: opacity var(--duration-normal);
	}

	.cta-button:hover {
		opacity: 0.9;
	}
</style>
